course: dictionary
lesson: 7416
title: Polarisation: one word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant. «Polarisation is when people split into two sides that hardly talk to each other.» Here the sentence is about people, so polarisation means a group splitting into two sides with opposite views.
explain: In a science lesson the same word means something else. Polarised sunglasses cut glare because they make light waves vibrate in only one direction. The words around the word (sunglasses, glare, light) are the clues.
explain: Ask yourself: what is the topic? Then swap your meaning into the sentence. «Common ground is what both sides agree on», so the opposite of polarisation is people finding common ground.
fact: Polarisation is a noun, and it is usually uncountable.
fact: «Polarisation» of people means a group splits into two sides with opposite views that move further apart.
fact: «Polarisation» of light means light waves are made to vibrate in only one direction.
fact: Polarised sunglasses use polarisation of light to cut glare.
fact: Phrases to know: «political polarisation» and «polarisation of light».
exercise: What does polarisation mean in «Polarisation is when people split into two sides that hardly talk to each other»? | Two poles of a magnet | A pair of sunglasses | *Two sides with opposite views moving further apart | Light waves vibrating in one direction
exercise: Polarised sunglasses cut glare. Which kind of polarisation do they use? | Polarisation of a school council | *Polarisation of light | Polarisation of people | Political polarisation
exercise: Find and copy the word from «Common ground is what both sides agree on» that shows what both sides share. | *agree | sides | ground | common
test: «A magnet has two opposite poles, and a battery gains opposite charges.» What does polarisation mean in this science sense? | Talking to each other a lot | Splitting into two sides with opposite views | *Gaining two opposite poles or charges | Light waves moving in one direction
test: Which sentence uses polarisation of people? | The sunglasses cut the glare off the sea | The battery was flat by lunchtime | The light turned into a single straight beam | *The two groups grew further apart and stopped listening to each other
exam: What is the best way to reduce polarisation in a group? | Ignore the group completely | Shout louder until the other side gives in | *Find common ground that both sides can agree on | Only listen to people who agree with you
